1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,235
I want my own smart speaker:

2
00:00:02,235 --> 00:00:04,530
a voice assistant that runs at home.

3
00:00:04,530 --> 00:00:06,506
The electronics are on my desk.

4
00:00:06,506 --> 00:00:08,100
What's missing is a case.

5
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:17,123
Today I'll show you how SOMA AI designs that case around the real board and speaker, with mic holes, a light ring and a grille, ready to print.

6
00:00:17,123 --> 00:00:18,478
No CAD knowledge needed.

7
00:00:23,550 --> 00:00:24,272
We use SOMA.

8
00:00:24,272 --> 00:00:28,240
It's free and open source, and you can download it at somacad.app.

9
00:00:29,190 --> 00:00:30,766
SOMA itself is free.

10
00:00:30,766 --> 00:00:33,524
For SOMA AI, you bring your own AI:

11
00:00:33,524 --> 00:00:36,440
a ChatGPT subscription or an API key.

12
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:42,921
The heart of it is the reSpeaker XVF3800 from Seeed Studio:

13
00:00:42,921 --> 00:00:50,360
four microphones, a ring of twelve LEDs, two buttons, and a small XIAO ESP32-S3 on the back.

14
00:00:53,617 --> 00:00:57,724
Seeed publishes a 3D file of the board on its wiki, under Creative Commons.

15
00:00:57,724 --> 00:01:01,667
They even have a flat case for it, but I want one with a speaker inside.

16
00:01:02,617 --> 00:01:04,187
So we download the 3D file.

17
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:10,428
In SOMA, we start a new model and import two files:

18
00:01:10,428 --> 00:01:15,620
the board's STEP file, and the datasheet of my speaker, a Visaton FRS 5 X.

19
00:01:18,183 --> 00:01:21,993
Click the STEP file, and SOMA shows the board, with the LED ring on top.

20
00:01:26,750 --> 00:01:29,200
Underneath sit the XIAO and the connectors.

21
00:01:34,033 --> 00:01:35,887
For the speaker there's no 3D file.

22
00:01:35,887 --> 00:01:38,323
But the datasheet has every dimension we need.

23
00:01:42,233 --> 00:01:44,175
Everything is prepared.

24
00:01:44,175 --> 00:01:46,625
Now we build it with SOMA AI:

25
00:01:46,625 --> 00:01:50,763
I click its tab, and pick the model, GPT-6.1 Sol.

26
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:56,835
First, I ask it to bring in the board, and to build the speaker from its datasheet.

27
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:08,211
SOMA AI reads the STEP file and the datasheet, measures both parts, and places them:

28
00:02:08,211 --> 00:02:11,427
the board on top, the speaker below it, facing forward.

29
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:13,177
Here they are.

30
00:02:13,177 --> 00:02:16,898
The structure tree lists the board with its parts, and the speaker.

31
00:02:16,898 --> 00:02:18,009
Both are references:

32
00:02:18,009 --> 00:02:19,730
see-through, and never printed.

33
00:02:22,867 --> 00:02:23,642
Now the case.

34
00:02:23,642 --> 00:02:26,025
I describe it the way I'd tell a friend:

35
00:02:26,025 --> 00:02:35,557
holes above the microphones, a light ring above the LEDs, a grille for the speaker, buttons and ports reachable, screws that match the board, and easy to print.

36
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:44,063
Before it starts, SOMA AI asks what is still open.

37
00:02:44,063 --> 00:02:46,150
I click my answers and send them.

38
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:52,133
Then it works through it step by step.

39
00:02:52,133 --> 00:02:57,590
SOMA builds the model after every change, and SOMA AI looks at pictures of its own result to check it.

40
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:01,455
And here's my smart speaker.

41
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:05,629
The structure tree shows what it made:

42
00:03:05,629 --> 00:03:12,608
the tower, a top cover, the front grille, the base and the white light ring, plus standard screws and heat-set inserts.

43
00:03:12,608 --> 00:03:16,010
The board and the speaker are still inside, as references.

44
00:03:18,967 --> 00:03:22,359
To check the fit, SOMA has tools that only change the view.

45
00:03:22,359 --> 00:03:23,912
Section cuts the case open:

46
00:03:23,912 --> 00:03:28,282
the board sits right under the top, and the speaker right behind its grille.

47
00:03:32,034 --> 00:03:32,883
A nice detail:

48
00:03:32,883 --> 00:03:38,404
SOMA AI noticed that the board has only three mounting holes, not four, and used all three.

49
00:03:42,734 --> 00:03:45,972
Explode, another view tool, shows how it goes together:

50
00:03:45,972 --> 00:03:52,624
the light ring and the cover on top, the grille in front, the base below, all held by screws in heat-set inserts.

51
00:03:56,634 --> 00:03:58,502
I'd like to hang it on the wall.

52
00:03:58,502 --> 00:04:02,764
So I turn it around, click Mark up, and draw where the keyhole should go.

53
00:04:03,714 --> 00:04:06,505
A circle for the screw head, and a slot above it.

54
00:04:06,505 --> 00:04:09,524
Then an arrow, and a short note that says what it is.

55
00:04:16,701 --> 00:04:19,071
Send it to SOMA AI, with one sentence.

56
00:04:30,867 --> 00:04:33,157
And there's the keyhole on the back, right where I drew it.

57
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:39,971
SOMA AI also turned the main sizes into parameters.

58
00:04:39,971 --> 00:04:42,210
You find them in the Parameters tab.

59
00:04:43,567 --> 00:04:45,649
A thicker wall, bigger grille holes:

60
00:04:45,649 --> 00:04:47,847
drag a slider, and the model rebuilds.

61
00:04:47,847 --> 00:04:49,582
The agent isn't even involved.

62
00:04:57,633 --> 00:05:00,243
Reset all brings back SOMA AI's values.

63
00:05:02,100 --> 00:05:03,336
Finally, Export.

64
00:05:03,336 --> 00:05:04,650
All parts as 3MF:

65
00:05:04,650 --> 00:05:08,359
one file per printed part, ready for the slicer.

66
00:05:08,359 --> 00:05:11,990
The board, the speaker and the screws stay out.

67
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:18,930
From a board on my desk to a case that's ready to print, without drawing a single line.

68
00:05:22,033 --> 00:05:25,272
This was a simple example, made quickly to show how SOMA works.

69
00:05:25,272 --> 00:05:28,563
With more time and planning, you can design something like this:

70
00:05:29,513 --> 00:05:35,795
a smart speaker with a fabric cover held in place by piping, several circuit boards, and an LED ring.

71
00:05:35,795 --> 00:05:37,163
Also designed in SOMA.

72
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:41,930
And here it is, built.

73
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:46,476
Under the lid sit the boards and the LED ring.

74
00:05:46,476 --> 00:05:50,290
Below, the printed shell, with the fabric held in its groove.

75
00:05:51,240 --> 00:05:53,450
Get SOMA for free at somacad.app.
